# CHANGES WITH 0.3.0:

        pico-init now boots on its own: the build installs the targets it
        and its units refer to. Packaging changes for existing images are
        called out below.

##        PID 1 and unit files:

        * The build installs default.target (a link to multi-user.target),
          multi-user.target, sysinit.target, sockets.target and
          bluetooth.target in lib/systemd/system. multi-user.target wants
          sockets.target, sysinit.target and bluetooth.target and runs after
          sysinit.target. Until now a distribution had to supply all of
          them, and PID 1 loaded no units without a default.target. A
          distribution with its own targets overwrites these files or
          points default.target elsewhere; docs/DISTRO_PORTING.md has the
          list of what the build ships and what the image still has to
          provide (getty units, the preset, base-files' volatile
          directories).

##        Build and tests:

        * test-shipped-units lints the installed units as one tree and
          checks what multi-user.target wants.

##        Contributions from:

        Koen Kooi

# CHANGES WITH 0.2.2:

        This release brings the command-line tools, the compatibility
        libraries and the code derived from systemd up to systemd v262.
        Behaviour that changes for existing images is called out below.

##        Command-line tools:

        * picoctl follows systemctl 262: list-units hides inactive units
          unless --all or --state= is given, list-unit-files and list-jobs
          take patterns, -o json, json-pretty, json-sse, json-seq and cat
          are available, and show takes -p, -P and --value. kill knows the
          full signal table, --kill-whom= and --kill-value=. New verbs are
          list-jobs, get-default, whoami, link, add-wants and add-requires;
          enable, disable, mask and preset take --now, and preset-all and
          --preset-mode= exist. The reboot, poweroff, halt and shutdown
          personalities parse the options of the real tools. Verbs and
          options the system cannot honour fail with a reason and a
          non-zero status instead of reporting success; docs/PICOCTL.md
          lists which are real, accepted and refused. A preset ignore rule
          now leaves the unit alone in picoctl preset, and list-unit-files
          shows ignored.

        * picolog follows journalctl 262: all sixteen output formats,
          --output-fields=, priority ranges, repeated -u with globs,
          FIELD=VALUE matches, -g, --since and --until, cursors, -b, -r,
          -n and -f, --file=, --list-boots, --disk-usage and --new-id128.
          Options that a RAM-only journal makes moot are accepted; options
          that would change state (--rotate, --verify, --user and others)
          exit with a diagnostic.

        * netctl follows networkctl 262: list, status and lldp take
          interface patterns, and label, up, down, delete, reconfigure,
          cat, mask, unmask and persistent-storage work. --no-legend, -a,
          -s, --json= and --runtime are available. dhcp-lease, renew,
          forcerenew and edit are refused with a reason.

        * pico-tmpfiles gains --cat-config, --tldr, --inline and --replace=;
          --dry-run, --user, --image and --purge are refused.
          pico-unit-lint takes -q, --root=, --instance=, --version and the
          systemd-analyze verify options that apply to it.

        * pico-init, devd, netd and logd accept the command lines of
          systemd, systemd-udevd, systemd-networkd and systemd-journald.
          PID 1 reads --unit=, systemd.unit=, the single, rescue,
          emergency and 1-5 words, --show-status, systemd.show_status=,
          systemd.gpt_auto= and fstab= from the kernel command line.
          Options that need machinery pico-init does not have are accepted
          and logged as ignored, or refused where ignoring them would
          change what runs.

##        PID 1 and unit files:

        * Exec*= words decode C escapes, keep unknown escapes, and join text
          glued to a closing quote, as systemd 262 does. EnvironmentFile=
          is read with shell-like quoting, escapes and line continuation.
          A line ending in an escaped backslash no longer continues, a
          malformed section header drops its section, and an assignment
          with an empty key is skipped. A preset ignore rule is a
          terminating rule, and preset patterns match without backslash
          escapes. Unterminated quotes in an EnvironmentFile= now run to
          the end of the file, as upstream.

        * Services start with a clean environment: PATH, TERM for
          tty-attached units, and USER, LOGNAME, HOME and SHELL for User=.
          They no longer inherit PID 1's environment or stray name=value
          words from the kernel command line. Environment= and
          EnvironmentFile= still override.

        * The root filesystem check reads fsck.mode=force|skip|auto and
          fsck.repair=preen|yes|no from the kernel command line.

        * os-release falls back to /usr/lib/os-release when
          /etc/os-release is missing.

##        netd:

        * A lease whose router lies outside the leased subnet gets a
          link-scope host route first, and a renewal that drops the router
          removes the default route the old lease installed.

        * DHCP= accepts every boolean spelling in either case, Name= in
          [Match] understands !pattern, and an empty Name=, DNS= or NTP=
          clears earlier values. DNS= and NTP= stop at 128 entries.

        * A reload requested after SIGTERM is dropped.

##        logd:

        * RFC 3164 timestamps in /dev/log datagrams are split off, so the
          syslog tag becomes SYSLOG_IDENTIFIER and the stamp is stored as
          SYSLOG_TIMESTAMP. Kernel-escaped bytes in /dev/kmsg messages are
          decoded, and a logd restart within one boot resumes after the last
          stored record instead of replaying the ring.

##        devd:

        * by-id links for USB serial devices carry the -port<N> suffix and
          omit the serial component when the device has no serial string,
          as udev names them.

##        libsystemd and libudev compatibility:

        * Both libraries follow systemd v262: libsystemd.so.0.45.0 and
          libudev.so.1.7.15, the LIBSYSTEMD_260, LIBSYSTEMD_261 and
          LIBSYSTEMD_262 symbol versions, and a pkg-config version of 262.

        * Every name in upstream's libsystemd symbol file is now exported.
          The sd_json_*, sd_varlink_* and sd_path_lookup* families are
          stubs: with no JSON or varlink machinery behind them, calls that
          need a real implementation return -EOPNOTSUPP and functions that
          take an object handle answer for a NULL handle as upstream does.
          sd_path_lookup() and sd_path_lookup_strv() return -EOPNOTSUPP for
          every type. The cpu and io pressure event sources fail like the
          memory pressure source, and sd_session_get_extra_device_access()
          reports that there is no logind.

##        Contributions from:

        Koen Kooi

# CHANGES WITH 0.2.1:

        A small release: unit files that ask for KillMode= are now honoured,
        and the repository gained reference documentation.

##        PID 1 and unit files:

        * KillMode= (control-group, mixed, process and none), KillSignal=,
          SendSIGHUP= and SendSIGKILL= are read from service units and shown
          by picoctl show. Previously every unit behaved as control-group:
          when the main process exited, the whole cgroup was killed. With
          process or none, processes the service left behind (a screen or
          tmux session started from an ssh login, for example) keep running
          when the main process exits and when the unit is stopped, and a
          stop does not wait for them. With mixed, the main process gets the
          signal first and the rest of the cgroup is killed afterwards. A
          stop that times out under none, or with SendSIGKILL=no, abandons
          the remaining processes instead of killing them.

        * A unit that is removed while processes still run in its cgroup
          hands the bare cgroup to the manager, which removes it once it is
          empty.

        * At shutdown, PID 1 sends SIGTERM to every remaining process, waits
          up to five seconds and then sends SIGKILL before the final sync,
          as systemd-shutdown does. Processes left behind by KillMode=process
          or none units are therefore no longer cut off by the reboot.

##        Miscellaneous:

        * The docs directory holds reference documentation modelled on
          systemd's: the architecture and boot sequence, netd, logd and devd,
          the compatibility libraries, differences from systemd, presets,
          socket activation, cgroup delegation, distro porting, debugging,
          coding style and the release process.

        * The project URL compiled into the binaries points at the
          Angstrom-distribution organisation on GitHub.

##        Contributions from:

        Koen Kooi

# CHANGES WITH 0.2.0:

        This is the first tagged release of pico-init, a small
        systemd-compatible init system for embedded Linux boards with around
        64 MiB of RAM. Everything below is new in this release; it describes
        what the release does rather than how it got there.

##        PID 1 and unit files:

        * pico-init runs as PID 1 on the cgroup v2 hierarchy with an sd_event
          main loop. It mounts /proc, /sys, /dev, /dev/pts, /dev/shm, /run,
          /sys/fs/cgroup and /sys/kernel/config, creates the /dev/fd and
          /dev/std{in,out,err} links, sets the hostname from /etc/hostname,
          and never exits: a fatal error freezes with the reason on the
          console instead of panicking the kernel.

        * Unit files are read from /etc/systemd/system and
          /usr/lib/systemd/system, /etc taking precedence. Service, socket
          and target units are supported, with .wants/.requires directories,
          symlink aliases, masking, template instances and the %n, %N, %p, %i
          and %I specifiers. Presets are applied on first boot only, detected
          the way systemd does it (/etc/machine-id missing, empty or
          "uninitialized"), and /etc/machine-id is created then.

        * [Unit] honours Description=, Requires=, Wants=, Before=, After=,
          Conflicts=, ConditionPathExists= and ConditionPathIsDirectory=,
          including '!' negation.

        * [Service] honours Type=simple, exec, oneshot, forking and notify;
          ExecCondition=, ExecStartPre= (every line, in order), ExecStart=,
          ExecStop= and ExecStopPost=, with the '-' modifier, single and
          double quoting, and $VAR/${VAR} expansion at spawn time;
          Environment= and EnvironmentFile=; User=, Group= and
          SupplementaryGroups=; PIDFile= and GuessMainPID=; Restart= with
          every systemd value, RestartSec=, RemainAfterExit=,
          TimeoutStartSec= and TimeoutStopSec= with systemd's defaults.

        * StandardInput=socket, tty, tty-force and tty-fail, TTYPath=, and
          StandardOutput=/StandardError= inherit, journal, syslog (and their
          +console forms) and null are honoured. Service output goes to logd
          by default.

        * Socket units support ListenStream=, ListenDatagram=, Service=,
          SocketMode=, Backlog=, Accept=yes per-connection instances with
          MaxConnections=, and StandardInput=socket for those instances.
          Services are ordered after sockets.target.

        * A unit that uses directives pico-init cannot honour still loads.
          Each dropped directive is recorded on the unit, PID 1 logs one
          warning per unit and a summary once the boot has settled, and
          "picoctl show" reports the list as Degraded-Directives=.
          Type=dbus and Type=idle run as simple, Type=notify-reload as
          notify, ExecReload= is stored but not run, and a dependency on an
          unsupported unit type such as .mount or .timer is recorded rather
          than followed. An unknown Type= still fails the unit.

        * A stop runs ExecStop=, then signals every process in the unit's
          cgroup, using cgroup.kill where the kernel provides it. A skipped
          ExecCondition= ends the start cleanly without triggering
          Restart=.

        * The sd_notify() socket is handled: READY=1 completes a
          Type=notify start, and STATUS=, ERRNO=, MAINPID= and BARRIER=1
          are honoured. Services get NOTIFY_SOCKET in their environment.

        * SIGHUP and "picoctl daemon-reload" re-read changed unit files, pick
          up new units and .wants/.requires links, and apply the new values
          from each unit's next start.

        * The boot console shows a version greeting and systemd-style
          OK, FAILED, TIME and DEPEND status lines.

        * PID 1 feeds /dev/watchdog0 (or /dev/watchdog) when present and
          leaves it armed through shutdown. Ctrl-Alt-Del triggers an orderly
          reboot.

        * The root filesystem is checked before anything writes to it and
          then remounted read-write following /etc/fstab's "/" line. A
          filesystem the checker could not repair stays read-only and the
          boot continues.

        * /etc/fstab is processed by PID 1 before any unit starts, using the
          kernel's new mount API. UUID=, LABEL=, PARTUUID= and PARTLABEL=
          are resolved by built-in readers for ext2/3/4, swap, FAT, exFAT,
          XFS, btrfs and NTFS, matching blkid's output. Swap lines are
          activated, noauto lines are left alone, and the outcome of every
          line is recorded in /run/pico-init/fstab.

        * MTD flash named in ubi.mtd= on the kernel command line is attached
          to UBI from userspace when UBI is a module, so ubifs fstab lines
          work either way.

        * Swap partitions on the boot disk are activated following the
          Discoverable Partitions Specification, unless /etc/fstab names
          any swap.

        * The wall clock is restored from /etc/timestamp before any service
          starts and saved two minutes after boot, every ten minutes and at
          shutdown. It only ever moves forward, to the latest of the saved
          time, the file's mtime and a build epoch.

##        cgroups:

        * Every service runs in its own cgroup below
          /sys/fs/cgroup/system.slice, watched through cgroup.events.

        * At boot PID 1 enables the cpu and memory controllers in the root
          cgroup.subtree_control and in system.slice, so unit cgroups have
          cpu.weight, memory.high and similar files. This is best effort: a
          controller the kernel refuses is logged as a warning.

        * Delegate= is parsed with systemd's semantics: a boolean or a list
          of controllers, an empty value delegates with no controllers, and
          "no" clears it. For a service with a non-root User=, the unit
          cgroup is chowned to that user.

        * DelegateSubgroup= is supported. With Delegate= set, the main
          process starts in <unit cgroup>/<name> (handed to a non-root
          User=), and every other process of the unit (ExecStartPre=,
          ExecStop=, ...) goes to <unit cgroup>/.control, so the unit cgroup
          holds no process and the service can enable controllers in it. An
          invalid name is ignored with a warning, and a subgroup that cannot
          be created falls back to the unit cgroup.

        * Child cgroups a service created are removed depth-first when the
          unit stops, and a stale, empty unit cgroup left by an earlier run
          is reset before the next start.

##        picoctl:

        * picoctl is the systemctl equivalent, with status, list-units,
          list-unit-files, show, cat, is-active, is-failed, is-enabled,
          is-system-running, enable, disable, reenable, preset, mask,
          unmask, start, stop, restart, try-restart, reload-or-restart,
          kill, reset-failed, daemon-reload, reboot, poweroff and halt.
          Options are parsed anywhere on the command line, and a bare unit
          name gets .service appended.

        * Output and colours follow systemctl. "picoctl status" without a
          unit prints the system overview, and with one it includes the
          unit's recent log lines and says "A dependency job for X failed"
          for a dependency failure.

        * "picoctl show" prints Delegate=, DelegateControllers= and
          DelegateSubgroup= alongside Degraded-Directives=.

        * The read verbs work from the state files PID 1 publishes in
          /run/pico-init/units and keep working when PID 1 is busy; the file
          verbs accept --root.

##        netd:

        * netd reads .network files from /etc/systemd/network,
          /run/systemd/network and /usr/lib/systemd/network, implementing
          [Match] Name=, [Network] DHCP=, Address=, Gateway=, DNS=, NTP=,
          DHCPServer=, LLDP=, EmitLLDP=, and [DHCPv4] UseDNS=, UseNTP= and
          UseTimezone=. Other directives are ignored. DHCP=ipv6 is accepted
          and does nothing.

        * A DHCPv4 client and server, lifted from systemd. The client
          applies DNS servers to /etc/resolv.conf, NTP servers from option
          42 and the time zone from option 101; a lease's address and router
          survive a reload.

        * An SNTP client sets the clock from NTP= or DHCP-provided servers.

        * netd speaks LLDP. LLDP=yes or LLDP=routers-only receives
          neighbour announcements, and EmitLLDP= (yes, nearest-bridge,
          non-tpmr-bridge, customer-bridge) sends them; both are off unless
          set. The parser rejects malformed frames whole, the neighbour
          table of each link is bounded, and neighbours are published in
          /run/netd/lldp.

        * A link whose kernel MAC address is random gets a stable one, and
          netd sets fq_codel (or fq, cake) as the default qdisc and bbr as
          TCP congestion control when the kernel offers them.

        * netctl is a networkctl-style client with status, list, lldp and
          reload. Apart from reload it does not need netd to be running.

##        logd:

        * logd collects service stdout/stderr, /dev/log and /dev/kmsg into a
          2 MiB ring in /run/logd, in journal export format. Its sockets are
          held by PID 1 through logd.socket, so a logd restart loses no
          output, and PID 1's own messages written before logd started are
          backfilled from the kernel log.

        * picolog is a journalctl-style reader of that ring, with
          journalctl's priority colours.

##        devd:

        * devd handles device events on images that run devtmpfs without
          udev: it maintains /dev/disk/by-partuuid, by-partlabel, by-uuid,
          by-label and by-id links, and mounts data partitions of
          GPT-formatted removable media under /media, never touching a
          device that is already mounted.

        * devd runs installed udev rules files, supporting ACTION,
          SUBSYSTEM, KERNEL, ENV{}, ATTR{}, ATTRS{}, MODE, OWNER, GROUP,
          SYMLINK+=, RUN+=, GOTO and LABEL. A rule using any other key is
          skipped whole, so a partly understood rule never matches more than
          intended.

        * devd loads kernel modules for a device's MODALIAS, and classifies
          input devices the way udev's input_id builtin does, recording
          ID_INPUT* properties in /run/udev/data.

##        libsystemd and libudev compatibility:

        * libsystemd.so.0 and libudev.so.1 are built with systemd v259's
          symbol versions, with libsystemd.pc, systemd.pc, libudev.pc and
          udev.pc checked against systemd's own templates.

        * sd-daemon, sd-event and sd-id128 are real implementations;
          sd-journal writes to logd and reads its ring; sd-device and
          libudev read sysfs with the udev database devd writes overlaid,
          and udev_monitor delivers real uevents; the cgroup queries of
          sd-login are answered from /proc.

        * Functions without a mechanism behind them return an error
          rather than a fake success: sd-bus never connects, there is no
          hwdb, and the logind queries report no sessions or seats.

##        Build and tests:

        * The build uses meson (0.62 or newer) and has no dependencies beyond
          the C library. The test suite runs with "meson test"; CI runs
          it as root, since it creates real cgroups and network devices.

        * pico-unit-lint runs PID 1's unit parser over an installed unit
          tree and reports every directive a booted system would drop, for
          use in image builds.

        * pico-tmpfiles implements systemd-tmpfiles --create, --clean and
          --remove over tmpfiles.d files, with systemd v259's grammar and
          file precedence.

##        Miscellaneous:

        * pico-init is GPL-2.0-or-later. The DHCP, SNTP and time code in
          netd and the vendored systemd symbol and pkg-config files are
          LGPL-2.1-or-later, and every file is REUSE compliant.

##        Contributions from:

        Koen Kooi
